<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Hadiya Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim state of the southern Ethiopian highlands that alternated between tribute to the Christian emperors and alliance with Adal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hadiya is first mentioned in Arabic and Ethiopian sources in the 13th century as a Muslim state southwest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Shewa</a>, on routes bringing slaves and goods north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>. Its people spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiyya-language">Hadiyya</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/highland-east-cushitic-languages">Highland East Cushitic</a> language. In 1332 its ruler Amano was defeated by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> and made tributary to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hadiya was ruled by a garad or sultan and supplied troops to the Christian emperors in return for autonomy. Its trade in slaves and horses made it valuable to both sides. Relations were tense: Garad Mahiko rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> in the 15th century, while a Hadiya princess, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleni-of-ethiopia">Eleni</a>, married the emperor and became a powerful empress dowager.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hadiya joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal</a> in the 1530s, and was then broken up by the Oromo migrations of the 16th century. The Hadiya people still live in southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, where a zone bears their name.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1250 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.58%;width:6.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amano, Mahiko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hadiyya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hadiya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1425&amp;focus=hadiya-sultanate" aria-label="Hadiya Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5724 1891 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hadiya Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6044 2147L6029 1964M6044 2147L6135 2044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0M6135 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6044 2147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6044 2147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1425&amp;focus=hadiya-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

